/// Calling a top-level function with the wrong number of arguments raises.
///
/// ALGOL-24.md, under Function and procedure declarations: "Calling with too
/// few arguments for the parameters that have no default, or with more than
/// there are parameters to take them, is an error: 'Expected 1 arguments but
/// got 0.'"  That message is listed under Run-time errors, so it is raised as a
/// String and a handler for String catches it.
///
/// ⚠️ THE COMPILED HALF DOES NOT CHECK AT ALL, and this is not a refusal that
/// arrives late -- it is an answer that is wrong.  Emitted code calls the
/// function with whatever arguments were written, missing parameters arrive as
/// nil, and a body that does not touch them returns normally.  The program
/// below prints 42 and exits 0 when compiled, and is refused when interpreted.
///
/// That makes it the shape of defect the differential suites exist to catch and
/// cannot: nothing in tests/programs/ calls a function with the wrong arity, so
/// the two halves never had occasion to disagree where anyone was looking.
///
/// ⚠️ Assertion on the MESSAGE and not merely on 'something was caught'.  A
/// handler that fires for the wrong reason would satisfy the weaker test, and
/// the sentence is the part a program can act on.
///
/// Interpreted: both tests pass.
/// Compiled:    both fail -- nothing is raised, so Caught is left empty.
///
function Three (A : Integer, B : Integer, C : Integer) : Integer;
begin
    // ⚠️ The body must NOT use its parameters.  An earlier draft summed them,
    // and compiled code then failed with 'Operands must be two numbers, or two
    // strings.' -- which looks like the arity being caught and is not.  It is
    // nil arriving in A and reaching '+'.  A body that ignores them is what
    // makes the silence visible.
    Exit 42;
end

test 'Too Few Arguments Raises';
begin
    var Caught := '';

    try
        Three (1);
    except
        on E : String do Caught := E;
    end

    AssertEqual ('Expected 3 arguments but got 1.', Caught);
end

test 'Too Many Arguments Raises';
begin
    var Caught := '';

    try
        Three (1, 2, 3, 4);
    except
        on E : String do Caught := E;
    end

    AssertEqual ('Expected 3 arguments but got 4.', Caught);
end
